---
import Base from '../layouts/Base.astro';
import { SITE, STATUS } from '../site';
---

<Base title="About" description={`About ${SITE.author} and how this garden works.`}>
  <article class="prose max-w-none pt-2 sm:pt-6">
    <h1>About</h1>
    <p>
      I'm <strong>{SITE.author}</strong>, a graduate student at Dartmouth working on generative
      models — currently multi-subject reference conditioning for diffusion transformers. This
      site is where I study in public.
    </p>
    <p>The name is the map. Everything here lives on one of three tracks:</p>
    <ul>
      <li><strong>Latents</strong> — diffusion, image &amp; video generation: mechanisms, conditioning, efficiency.</li>
      <li><strong>Logs</strong> — distributed systems (MIT 6.824) and Designing Machine Learning Systems.</li>
      <li><strong>Loops</strong> — agentic systems: tools, orchestration, evaluation.</li>
    </ul>
    <p>
      The interesting part is where they intersect: <em>the systems problems of generative
      models</em> — serving diffusion cheaply, scaling training, building agents that use these
      models well.
    </p>
    <h2>How this garden works</h2>
    <p>
      This is a <a href="https://maggieappleton.com/garden-history">digital garden</a>, not a
      blog. Posts are published early and tended over time, and each one wears its maturity
      honestly:
    </p>
    <ul>
      {
        Object.values(STATUS).map((s) => (
          <li>
            {s.emoji} <strong>{s.label}</strong> — {s.hint}.
          </li>
        ))
      }
    </ul>
    <p>
      If a seed looks half-finished, that's because it is — publishing early is the whole
      point.
    </p>
    <p>
      The daily <a href="/log">Reading Log</a> is the garden's heartbeat — one tiny entry
      about what I read and what stuck — while the posts here grow more slowly.
    </p>
    <h2>Elsewhere</h2>
    <p>
      <a href={SITE.github}>GitHub</a> · <a href="/rss.xml">RSS</a>
    </p>
  </article>
</Base>
